window.docContents[98].resolve({"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-and-Cartan-matrix-of-path-algebras/#Chapter9___Problem9___4___6":{"contents":"Problem 9.4.6. (i) Show that the path algebra P_Q of any quiver Q with at least one edge has homological dimension 1. In particular, the homological dimension of the free algebra k\\langle x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\rangle is 1 (for n \\geq 1).\n\n(ii) Let Q be a finite oriented graph without oriented cycles. Find the Cartan matrix of its path algebra P_Q.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.4. Homological dimension\tHomological dimension and Cartan matrix of path algebras","header":"Homological dimension and Cartan matrix of path algebras","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-and-Cartan-matrix-of-path-algebras/#Chapter9___Problem9___4___6"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Proof-of-Corollary-2___3___12/#Chapter2___Discussion_proof_Corollary2___3___12":{"contents":"Proof. Let V be irreducible. For any element a \\in A, the operator \\rho(a) : V \\to V is an intertwining operator. Indeed,\n\n\\rho(a)\\rho(b)v = \\rho(ab)v = \\rho(ba)v = \\rho(b)\\rho(a)v\n\n(the second equality is true since the algebra is commutative). Thus, by Schur's lemma, \\rho(a) is a scalar operator for any a \\in A. Hence every subspace of V is a subrepresentation. But V is irreducible, so 0 and V are the only subspaces of V. This means that \\dim V = 1 (since V \\neq 0). \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.3. Representations\tProof of Corollary 2.3.12","header":"Proof of Corollary 2.3.12","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Proof-of-Corollary-2___3___12/#Chapter2___Discussion_proof_Corollary2___3___12"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Transition-to-quiver-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___1":{"contents":"As another example consider the representation theory of quivers. A quiver is an oriented graph Q (which we will assume to be finite). A representation of Q over a field k is an assignment of a k-vector space V_i to every vertex i of Q and of a linear operator A_h : V_i \\to V_j to every directed edge h going from i to j (loops and multiple edges are allowed). We will show that a representation of a quiver Q is the same thing as a representation of a certain algebra P_Q called the path algebra of Q. Thus one may ask: what are the indecomposable finite dimensional representations of Q?\n\nMore specifically, let us say that Q is of finite type if it has finitely many indecomposable representations.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.1. What is representation theory?\tTransition to quiver representation theory","header":"Transition to quiver representation theory","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Transition-to-quiver-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___10":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.10. Frobenius determinant\tSection 4.10: Frobenius determinant — group determinant setup","header":"Section 4.10: Frobenius determinant — group determinant setup","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___10"}});
